For the lab, use a disposable landing page and a second static page that contains only fictional fields and a clear exercise banner. The following demonstration still models the behavior of a takeover closely enough for trainees to inspect the evidence.

Place a script near the end of the landing page. It waits briefly so the initial page can render, reads the referrer category, selects a corresponding training destination, and replaces the current location. The example below assumes every destination is hosted on the lab machine and that the pages contain no credential collection or external network calls.

    const trainingTargets = {
      search: '/exercise/login-search.html',
      partner: '/exercise/login-partner.html',
      direct: '/exercise/login-direct.html'
    };

    const source = document.referrer || '';
    let bucket = 'direct';
    try {
      const host = new URL(source).hostname;
      if (host.endsWith('search.example.test')) bucket = 'search';
      if (host.endsWith('partner.example.test')) bucket = 'partner';
    } catch (ignored) {}

    setTimeout(() => {
      window.location.replace(trainingTargets[bucket]);
    }, 1800);

The lab operator can serve the pages from a local web server and create harmless links whose referrers end in the reserved test domain. The look-alike page should display a large TRAINING ONLY notice, accept no data, and immediately discard any submitted form. In a real incident, the same pattern might be disguised with a less visible transition, a path that looks like an account route, or conditions based on device and campaign source; that is why the exercise asks trainees to examine timing, referrer use, navigation history, and the network trace rather than only the final screen.

To make the exercise observable, have the landing page log a local event such as redirect-start with the selected bucket, and have the destination log redirect-arrival. Keep those logs inside the isolated lab. Ask trainees to detect the behavior using browser developer tools, a content-security-policy report, a static search for navigation calls, and a comparison against the known-good build. Then remove the script and verify that the page no longer navigates unexpectedly.

Do not copy this demonstration into a production site, use a real brand, send traffic outside the lab, request passwords, or use it against people who have not consented. The point of the exercise is to show how a small client-side change can create a deceptive navigation chain and how defenders can identify it, not to operate a phishing page.

The staging copy was bound to a private hostname, and the researcher placed the experiment behind a feature flag so the behavior could be enabled for a test cohort. The code was small enough to hide among ordinary analytics calls:

    const source = document.referrer || '';
    const cohort = source.includes('campaign.example.test');
    const target = 'https://account-mirror.example.invalid/session';
    if (cohort && !location.pathname.startsWith('/exercise/')) {
      setTimeout(() => window.location.replace(target), 1400);
    }

The destination was a static imitation hosted in the same isolated exercise environment. It contained no real brand assets, accepted no input, and displayed a training notice after the page loaded. The researcher used a reserved fictional domain so a copy of the scene could not accidentally send anyone to an actual site. A test link supplied the referrer condition; a direct visit to the staging home page remained normal. Browser developer tools showed the landing page render, the delayed navigation, and the replacement of the history entry.

For the story, the important part was the chain of evidence. The engineer captured the template before and after the edit, the feature-flag change, the deployment identity, the browser network trace, the destination request, and the staging server's local event log. The selective branch explained why the owner did not see the redirect from a bookmark. The delayed navigation explained why a screenshot of the first page looked harmless. The suspicious external-looking string, even though reserved for the exercise, explained why static analysis and content-security-policy reports mattered.

The researcher then disabled the flag, removed the experiment, and compared the staging build with the signed baseline. The clone discarded every submitted form and generated no external request. The team verified that a clean browser stayed on the landing page for direct, search-like, and campaign-like test links after the change was removed. They also reviewed the risks of history replacement, conditional referrer checks, and a copied sign-in appearance as incident indicators.

The scene includes an explicit authorization boundary: the researcher had written permission, isolated traffic, reserved domains, synthetic accounts, and a teardown checklist. No production visitor, real brand, credential, or third-party host was involved. The exercise teaches how a quiet client-side change can create a deceptive navigation chain and how to investigate it; the same code would be abusive if moved outside that controlled staging copy.
